print("seaborn installed and imported successfully.")"}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Fish productivity per unit area has a non-linear (hump-shaped) relationship with depth, peaking at intermediate depths.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a quadratic relationship between depth and productivity per square meter across all reef‐year observations.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute productivity density: for each reef‐year‐depth, sum the 'prod' across replicates and divide by total surveyed area (250 m² × number of transects).  2. Assemble a dataset of (depth, productivity_density) pairs.  3. Fit two regression models: (a) linear: productivity_density ~ depth; (b) quadratic: productivity_density ~ depth + depth².  4. Compare model fits (AIC, R²) to assess whether the quadratic term significantly improves the fit (use likelihood ratio test or ANOVA).  5. Plot predicted productivity_density vs depth with observed points and fitted curve.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of model coefficients, standard errors, p‐values for linear and quadratic terms. 2. Model comparison metrics (AIC, ΔAIC, R²). 3. Visualization of observed vs. fitted productivity_density across depths showing hump‐shaped pattern if present."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher mean species richness exhibit lower inter‐annual variability in total fish productivity, consistent with a diversity–stability relationship.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether average species richness per reef predicts reduced coefficient of variation (CV) in annual total productivity at that reef.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compute species richness (count of unique 'species') and total productivity (sum of 'prod' over all depths and replicates).  2. For each reef, calculate: (a) mean richness across years, (b) CV of total productivity across years (std/mean).  3. Fit a linear regression: productivity_CV ~ mean_richness.  4. Test the slope coefficient for significance (t‐test).  5. Check for outliers and leverage; potentially use robust regression.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Scatterplot of productivity CV vs. mean richness with fitted regression line. 2. Regression summary (slope, p‐value, R²). 3. Table of reefs’ mean richness and productivity CV values."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Mean fish body size has declined over the survey period, and the magnitude of this decline differs among regions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify temporal trends in mean fish size per reef by region, and test for region × year interaction.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute mean size: for each reef–year, average the 'size' of all observations.  2. Create a dataset with columns (reef, year, region, mean_size).  3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: mean_size ~ year * region + (1 | reef).  4. Test the significance of the interaction terms (year:region).  5. Plot region‐specific regression lines of mean_size vs. year.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary with fixed‐effect estimates and p‐values, particularly year × region interactions. 2. Plot of mean size over time stratified by region. 3. Table of per‐region trend slopes and confidence intervals."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Community‐level productivity‐to‐size ratio (sum(prod)/sum(size)) decreases as mean fish size increases across reef–year observations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether reefs dominated by larger fish yield lower productivity per unit summed length.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, calculate: (a) total prod_ts = sum(prod), (b) total_len = sum(size), and (c) P_L_ratio = prod_ts / total_len.  2. Compute mean_size per reef–year (prod‐weighted or simple mean).  3. Fit a regression model: P_L_ratio ~ mean_size (linear or log–log).  4. Test for significance and examine residuals.  5. Optionally, fit a non‐linear model if the relationship is curved.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression output (slope, p‐value, R²). 2. Scatterplot of P_L_ratio vs mean_size with fitted line. 3. Summary table of P_L_ratio and mean_size by reef–year."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher mean species richness have more temporally autocorrelated (stable) productivity time‐series, as indicated by larger AR(1) coefficients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Estimate the AR(1) temporal autocorrelation of total productivity per reef and relate it to average species richness.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, assemble its total annual productivity time‐series.  2. Fit an AR(1) model to each reef’s time‐series and extract the lag‐1 autocorrelation coefficient (ϕ).  3. Also compute mean annual richness for each reef.  4. Test for correlation between ϕ and mean richness (Spearman or linear regression).  5. Visualize the relationship with a scatterplot, highlighting reefs by data length.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef‐level AR(1) coefficients and mean richness. 2. Correlation/regression results (coefficient, p‐value). 3. Plot of ϕ vs mean richness."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Depth stratification explains more variance in productivity than inter‐annual variability when partitioning total variance via mixed‐effects modeling.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare variance components attributable to reef, year, and depth for productivity observations.",  
      "steps":"1. Use the full transect‐level dataset with columns (reef, year, depth, prod).  2. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: prod ~ 1 + (1|reef) + (1|year) + (1|depth).  3. Extract variance components for each random effect.  4. Compute the proportion of total variance explained by each component.  5. Rank the components to identify the dominant source of variance.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of variance components and their proportions. 2. Bar chart of variance proportions by grouping factor. 3. Interpretation note about the dominant drivers of productivity variance."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Species compositional turnover between depths (spatial β‐diversity) within a year exceeds turnover between consecutive years (temporal β‐diversity) within the same depth.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare within‐reef species dissimilarity across depths versus across years for each depth stratum.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compile presence‐absence matrices of species at shallow vs deep depths (aggregating replicates).  2. Compute Jaccard dissimilarity between shallow and deep communities (spatial turnover).  3. For each reef–depth combination, compute Jaccard dissimilarity between year t and t+1 communities (temporal turnover).  4. Assemble paired lists of (spatial, temporal) dissimilarities for each reef–year.  5. Perform a paired test (Wilcoxon or t‐test) to compare spatial vs temporal turnover.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of spatial and temporal dissimilarities (mean, median). 2. Paired test result (test statistic, p‐value). 3. Boxplots of spatial vs temporal turnover."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"At the reef–year–depth scale, the skewness of transect‐level productivity is inversely related to local species richness: less diverse communities have more uneven productivity among transects.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether productivity distributions become more skewed when species richness is low at a given reef–year–depth.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year–depth, collect the 4 transect‐level 'prod' values.  2. Compute: (a) species richness at that reef–year–depth (number of unique species across transects), (b) skewness of the 4 prod values.  3. Compile a dataset of (richness, skewness).  4. Fit a regression: skewness ~ richness (possibly log‐transformed richness).  5. Evaluate model fit and plot skewness vs richness with fitted line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary (coefficients, p‐value, R²). 2. Scatterplot of skewness vs richness. 3. Table of richness and skewness by reef–year–depth."  
    }  
  }  
]}
